[512.028957, "i", "cat << 'EOF' > design_primers.py\nimport subprocess\n\ndef get_tm(seq):\n    cmd = [\"oligotm\", \"-tp\", \"1\", \"-sc\", \"1\", \"-mv\", \"50\", \"-dv\", \"2\", \"-n\", \"0.8\", \"-d\", \"500\", seq]\n    res = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True)\n    return float(res.stdout.strip())\n\nwith open('sequences.fasta') as f:\n    lines = f.read().splitlines()\n\nseqs = {}\ncurr = None\nfor line in lines:\n    if line.startswith('>'):\n        curr = line[1:]\n        seqs[curr] = []\n    else:\n        seqs[curr].append(line)\n\nfor k in seqs:\n    seqs[k] = ''.join(seqs[k])\n\n# Let's define the template sequences for amplification for each part:\n# 1. input backbone: template is seqs['input']. \n# In input, the backbone to amplify goes from index 690 to 214 (wrapping around).\n# So input template for PCR is input[690:] + input[:214]\n# 2. egfp: seqs['egfp'] (without stop codon, or full egfp? Wait, egfp template sequence in sequences.fasta has stop codon, but the part assembled doesn't. When PCR amplifying egfp from the egfp template, the forward primer anneals at the start of egfp and reverse primer anneals at the end of egfp. If we want the PCR product to lack the stop codon, the reverse primer can just anneal slightly upstream of the stop codon or include the truncated end!)\n# Let's check egfp template: len is 717. Stop codon is last 3 bases (taa). If reverse primer anneals right before taa, or if the primer encodes the overhang without taa, that's standard for Golden Gate!)\n\nprint(\"Templates ready.\")\nEOF\npython3 design_primers.py\n"]
